       :raw
       "Unary `(integer? value)` returns Bool true exactly when the normally evaluated\nvalue belongs to an integer family defined by L5, and false for other normally\nevaluated values. It classifies the Gravity value family, not whether a\nconversion could produce an integer. The operand evaluates once under L2;\nits effects remain visible and a control transfer aborts invocation.\nClassification itself is pure and does not convert, allocate, mutate or\nevaluate stored payloads. It establishes no width, range, arithmetic-mode or\noverflow proof. Numeric-family semantics belong to `gravity.math`; an existing\n`gravity.core` compatibility spelling changes neither that ownership nor\nprofile and target legality.\n"}
      {:facet
       {:id "stdlib/facet/std5/336-865",
        :document-id "STD5",
        :source-occurrence-id
        "STD5/source/93ecfaa51ad98c9f46324680e196393c5e4275307e56d741349e69ca1e7072c8",
        :kind :acceptance-rule,
        :start-byte 336,
        :end-byte 865,
        :raw-sha256
        "247c111f8c6be28d14cf68372a529e86904e214f4bffd669b8401c47dc3c99eb"},
       :path
       "docs/phase-16-standard-library/215-std5-numeric-and-math-library-specification.md",
       :ref "86747916c4c876d707a1548113409fb939d854c9",
       :file-sha256
       "8ec0481d0c4314bf267aec3518836660ff25461da1e26435ff31ded3ccdbb8d6",
       :raw
       "`gravity.math` defines checked numeric operations, numeric tower APIs, elementary functions, approximate math, interval math, and EFIR-facing math construction.\nIt gives ordinary code convenient arithmetic while preserving the PDF requirement that safety, precision, domain, target lowering, and proof artifacts remain visible.\nThe library does not treat fast math as an implicit compiler switch.\nEvery loss of precision, overflow mode, domain restriction, approximation, and target-specific lowering must be selected or proven.\n"}
